# Draw.io diagrams

This project opted in to the user-level draw.io formatting rule.

Formatting only. Apply when creating or editing `.drawio` files in this project.

## Connectors

- Route in gutters. Do not run a line through unrelated icons.
- Leave space between neighbouring groups so lines travel in the gap, not over borders.
- Do not share a path segment with another arrow. Crossing at a right angle is allowed.
- Last waypoint must not sit on the target’s edge. That twists the arrowhead or hides it in the icon.
- Side entry: last segment is horizontal, about 18px off the icon, same Y as `entryY`.
- Top or bottom entry: last segment is vertical, about 16px off the icon, same X as `entryX`.
- Use `jettySize=0;entryPerimeter=1;exitPerimeter=1;endSize=8`.

Bad — last point on the target edge (head twists up the side):

```xml
entryX=0;entryY=0.5
<mxPoint x="1848" y="480" /> <!-- x is the left edge of the icon -->
```

Good — approach, then enter horizontally:

```xml
jettySize=0;entryPerimeter=1;exitPerimeter=1;endSize=8;entryX=0;entryY=0.5
<mxPoint x="1830" y="480" />
<mxPoint x="1830" y="436" />
```

## HTML tables

Match Hop Reference tables `htable` and `hinv`:

- Rectangle. `overflow=fill`. `verticalAlign=top`. `rounded=0;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none`.
- One-line HTML. No `&#xa;` between `<tr>` tags.
- Box height ≈ row count × 30px. All rows show. No empty band above or below.
- After a height change, move footnotes under the table, not inside the box.
- Do not use `text;html=1;overflow=width` for multi-row tables. Newlines in that mode push the table down and clip rows.

Bad:

```xml
style="text;html=1;overflow=width;..."
value="&lt;table&gt;&#xa;&lt;tr&gt;..."
```

Good:

```xml
style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;overflow=fill;fillColor=none;strokeColor=none;verticalAlign=top;spacing=0;"
value="&lt;table style=&quot;width:100%;height:100%;...&quot;&gt; &lt;tr&gt;...&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/table&gt;"
```

## After XML edits

Tell the user: close diagrams.net without saving, then reopen the `.drawio` file. An open editor can write the old geometry back.